WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:51.660
(Casting)

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:06.700
The rivalry between KT and SKT, two of South Korea’s largest telecom companies,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.160
is one of the oldest in esports.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.240
Dubbed “The Telecom War,” it has defined generations of players and shaped the history

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.060
of entire games.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:25.080
In Korea, the crucible from which esports emerged, no teams have battled for longer

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.090
than SK Telecom T1 and KT Rolster.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.340
It is a battle that has raged for years.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.660
In stadiums, on TV.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:42.480
The Telecom War was foundational for an industry that, while still niche in the West, was already

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.880
in full swing in South Korea.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:05.940
For SKT, it all began with Lim “Boxer” Yo Hwan, one of the greatest Stacraft: Brood

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.600
War players of all time.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:20.880
(Casting)

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:26.540
Boxer was a godfather of Korean esports, and years later people would call him the first

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:27.580
“Bonjwa.”

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.700
It’s a Korean pronoun meant to raise one’s self up that’s used to describe a Starcraft

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.200
player who the community agrees is the most dominant player of his era.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:44.200
Initially, Boxer founded a team named Orion in 2002 and in December 2003, the team’s

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.700
name changed to 4 Union.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:53.740
But in 2004, SK Telecom sponsored the org and SK Telecom T1 was born.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:03:02.340
If SKT was built around Boxer in the early 2000s, KT centered around Hong "YellOw" Jin Ho.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:11.060
Created as Raptors in 1998, the organization became the KTF MagicNs in March 2001.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:17.760
The org would change its name twice more in 2009 before settling on KT Rolster.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:23.420
KT became known for their willingness to spend big money on their roster.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:30.040
YellOw was known for his aggressive Zerg playstyle, and had one of the best ZvTs in the business.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:36.480
He was a frequent finalist in OSL from 2001-2005, though he never won.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:38.680
His biggest obstacle?

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:40.320
Boxer.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:52.420
YellOw and Boxer’s rivalry would become one of the game’s biggest, with KT and SKT’s

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:58.440
stars frequently butting heads on the game’s biggest stages, even before SKT was founded.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:04.540
And though their lifetime record was fairly close, Boxer always seemed to clinch the matches

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:05.860
that mattered most.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:28.200
(Casting)

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:34.400
But even as SKT and KT’s players competed for trophies in the individual competitions,

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.340
a new battlefield loomed.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.640
One that would become one of Starcraft’s most popular and influential, and one that

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.940
would set the framework for modern esports as we know it.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:45.740
Proleague.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.660
The team-based league became one of the game’s most popular competitive staples, and the

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:03.320
format — which saw both 1v1 and 2v2 matches
— would go on to define the Telecom War.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.800
The format for the 2005 SKY Proleague was simple.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:12.950
Two rounds, each with a winner, then a Grand Final in which the best teams from both rounds

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:14.780
face off for all the glory.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.900
Since SKT won both Round 1 and Round 2, the top three teams from both played in the Grand

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.020
Final tournament.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.000
But the final two teams came down to fate.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:37.600
Played in front of a packed stadium, the SKY Proleague Grand Finals in 2005 was the biggest

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.200
early competition in the Telecom War.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:47.110
And, much like many battles between SKT and KT that were yet to come, KT couldn’t overcome

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:48.400
their rivals.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:06:07.840
(Casting)

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:13.640
After the 2005 finals, both teams entered a period of decline.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:20.020
KTF’s top players were getting old, and many retired or were forced to enter the Air Force.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.320
In 2006, Boxer announced that he’d also received his draft letter, and would leave

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.760
his team for the Air Force.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:31.960
Then, in 2007, everything changed.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:35.360
His name is Lee “Flash” Young Ho.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:40.040
And he is widely seen as the best Starcraft
player of all time.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:43.840
He was only 14 when he made his professional debut.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:59.440
(Casting)

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:02.320
Flash was more than just a young prodigy.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:09.320
He went on a record-setting period of dominance in the months after his first OSL win in 2008.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:12.060
And that wouldn’t be the last record he set.

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:19.860
Flash was so good that he almost single-handedly skewed the Proleague results between SKT and KT.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:26.220
Where SKT dominated early bouts between the rival organizations, KT routinely got the

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.860
upper hand prior to the release of Starcraft 2 and the end of KeSPA-era Brood War.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:40.680
KT won Proleague in the 2009-2010 season and in the 2010-2011 Season, beating SKT to do

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.220
it both times.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:49.700
And though SKT did manage to topple KT in 2011-2012 Season 1, Flash’s dominance in

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:54.800
the OSL, MSL and other individual competitions could not be matched.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:08.280
But by 2012, the Korean esports scene was changing dramatically.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:14.860
The release of Starcraft 2 in July 2010 saw a shift in the community.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:19.960
And though many players continued to play Brood War, the new blood and major international

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:24.320
esports dollars flowing into SC2 divided the scene.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:29.820
There was one other factor: League of Legends.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.440
Though the game had been out for some time in the West, the release of a Korean server

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.260
was a watershed moment for LoL.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:43.280
And, with the time of Brood War beginning to wane, both KT and SKT made the jump to

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:46.140
League of Legends in late 2012.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:54.320
KT created KT Rolster A and B, while SKT acquired a roster that would go on to become SK Telecom

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:56.520
T1 #1 .

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:06.660
In February 2013, SKT created their second team, SK Telecom T1 #2 (later T1 K), which

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:11.080
they built around a promising rookie named Lee “Faker” Sang-hyeok.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:18.760
KT were quick to make a name for themselves, with a strong showing in Champions Winter 2012-13.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:23.040
And both orgs put up strong results in the Spring.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:30.560
But they were both on the upswing and by Summer 2013, KT and SKT were the teams to beat.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:46.940
Faker had matured since his rookie tournament, and was beginning to make a case for himself

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.280
as one of the best players in the world.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:54.840
And the Bullets’ talent-filled roster also boasted players who were beginning to cement

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:56.440
their legacies.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:02.720
And on a rainy day in August, in a huge field near the Jamsil Olympic Stadium in Seoul,

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:06.920
the two orgs clashed in the finals for the first time in League.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:13.280
Though SKT had individual star power, KT were seen as having stronger team play.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.280
And they got off to a strong start,

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:26.960
(Casting)

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:29.840
But SKT didn't back down.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:45.540
(Casting)

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.320
It all came down to a pivotal Game 5.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:53.600
With both Faker and Ryu blind-picking Zed, KT managed to get a small lead early.

00:10:53.600 --> 00:11:11.360
(Casting)

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:14.160
But SKT would not be denied.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:20.000
And just past the 30-minute mark, SKT’s first Champions title was all but secured.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:28.600
(Casting)

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:31.380
But Faker, wasn't done.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:55.460
(Casting)

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:01.120
The most famous outplay in the history of League was the icing on the cake for SKT.

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:06.140
And they completed the reverse sweep, in what went down as one of the greatest series of

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:08.200
League ever played.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:14.160
It was a new game, but for the rival orgs, there were shades of Brood War’s early days.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:15.640
KT were good.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:17.700
But not quite good enough.

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:23.400
Because of the way Worlds seeding worked at the time, second place was not good enough

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:26.280
to secure KT a berth to Worlds.

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:29.880
Worlds that year was SKT’s to win.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.800
And, though they still had their doubters...

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:45.380
"All aboard the Korean hype train, SKT T1 for the championship hands down. I've got my tickets here.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:48.780
I've got tickets to the Korean hype train. And I will be giving them out."

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:52.360
"Oh thank you Monte I appreciate you're like you're SKT favourite but I think it's...

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.000
or time for a revolution!"

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.800
"Thanks coach. No thanks. I'm going with Royal."

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:02.280
"I'm going to have to go ahead and choose Royal as well."

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:05.280
...Royal Club were no match.

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:25.000
(Casting)

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:28.860
SKT returned home victorious and unstoppable.

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:33.200
They didn’t drop a single game in the Group Stage of Champions Winter.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:39.740
They swept Samsung Blue in the quarterfinals, and secured a rematch against the Bullets in semis.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.640
This time it wasn’t close.

00:13:42.760 --> 00:14:08.680
(Casting)

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:14.480
SKT swept their rivals and cruised through the finals as well, completing what is still

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:22.580
the only perfect season in Korean League history, going a combined 15-0 across six series.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:31.020
If KT had dominated the end of Brood War, this new game, this new era, was SKT’s to claim.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:35.760
They had the best player in the world, and a strong enough lineup to crush the game’s

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:37.420
toughest competition.

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:41.080
League of Legends was theirs to dominate.

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:42.560
Until it wasn't.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:48.180
"Interesting to note that K has looked shaky in the past couple of weeks. I mean as shaky as they've ever looked

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:48.720
I should say."

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:51.360
"Well they looked lazy in their match against Optimus."

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:54.360
"I feel like Faker's picks, have been a bit cocky.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:58.600
"Fear in the eyes of Faker, that is terror right there."

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:03.300
"I can't believe we're seeing, SKT K lose a game here.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:07.100
"Or a team that didn't lose any games for an entire tournament last season."

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:13.600
2014 was a rough year for SKT, especially the T1K roster.

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:18.940
They placed Top 8 in both Spring and Summer, but losses in the Regional Finals saw them

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:21.300
fail to qualify for Worlds.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:28.280
Meanwhile, the T1 S roster, featuring the likes of MaRin, Easyhoon, Bang and Wolf, finished

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:30.280
fourth in the Summer Split.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:35.780
Both KT rosters were also unsuccessful in the Regional Finals.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:41.120
There was no question Faker was a strong player, or that KT could compete.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:46.080
But to some, it seemed like the Telecom teams’ time had come and gone.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:52.060
League was still relatively young, especially compared to the lifespan of Brood War in Korea.

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:58.160
But with an explosion of esports worldwide, and the existing talent pool in Korean orgs,

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:00.240
the scene had matured quickly.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:04.100
"And that's really what separates Korea as a region from a lot of the other regions.

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:09.060
"We're trying to get there infrastructure wise. But they have multiple sets of coaches."

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:12.080
"The Korean teams they know exactly how to punish mistakes, can

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:15.180
Taipei do the same, can Fnatic, can Cloud9 do the same thing here."

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:20.860
Some analysts still see 2014 as the most competitive year in Korea’s history.

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:26.180
And it seemed like the game was entering a new era, one dominated by Samsung.

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:28.840
But it didn’t happen that way.

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:34.180
With a huge influx of money into the Chinese scene, all 10 of Samsung’s starting players

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:37.020
were spirited away by lucrative contracts.

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:43.140
Many other players departed for other regions, which at the time could offer contracts larger

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:44.920
than many Korean orgs.

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:50.800
Dubbed the Great Korean Exodus, it remains the single greatest redistributing of talent

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:52.240
in the game’s history.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:58.500
2015 saw other changes, with the abolishing of sister teams and a move towards a league

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:03.360
system, rather than the tournament format Champions had been running since 2012.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:09.060
Between an amalgamation of talent onto single rosters and the departure of some of the region’s

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:13.340
most dominant players, it was a reset for the scene in many ways.

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:17.440
And both SKT and KT looked to capitalize.

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:26.440
"SKT T1. An organization that won Season 3 Worlds. And they still do have Bengi and Faker

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:29.900
so two of that Worlds team roster is still on this."

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.320
"KT looks like they finally gone with their starting lineup and that will be Score in the jungle.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:38.640
and of course with Score's progress in the jungle it should be very exciting to see those games."

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:43.540
A  new force on the scene, the GE Tigers, topped the regular season.

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:51.740
But SKT’s new, combined roster, featuring MaRin, Bengi, Easyhoon, Faker, Bang and Wolf,

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:56.000
was too much come playoff time, and they swept the finals.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:18:37.200
(Casting)

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:40.320
KT didn’t make playoffs.

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:45.680
SKT stumbled at MSI, but regrouped in time for summer.

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:53.320
In playoffs, hungry for another domestic title, a surging SKT and KT met in the finals for

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:56.720
the first time since Summer 2013.

00:18:57.100 --> 00:19:02.260
"So exciting because of the stories of these organizations. SKT vs KT,

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:07.320
it's been a decade of rivalry between these two organizsations across games."

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:12.920
"And we have what I consider to be the best best of five ever played in League of Legends history

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:14.400
in Summer 2013."

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:18.060
But in the end, KT were no match.

00:19:18.220 --> 00:19:25.360
The rest of the year and 2016, along with much of 2017, would be about one team and

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:26.640
one team only.

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:32.300
"SK Telecom!"

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:38.160
"SKT will be your first ever, two-time world champions!"

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:46.540
SKT 3-0's them in the playoffs on their way to their 2016 Spring title.

00:19:46.700 --> 00:20:20.540
(Casting)

00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:26.280
By Summer 2016, people were starting to think of the Telecom War as just a remainder of

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:27.680
a bygone era.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:34.920
The fans KT earned with their showings in 2013 and 2014 had become a punchline.

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:38.020
To be a KT fan was to know heartbreak.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:21:11.520
(Casting)

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:15.760
But after fighting their way to the second round of playoffs that summer.

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:20.860
KT earned themselves another crack at the unassailable SKT.

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:27.840
"It is definitely going to be a barn burner, very exciting KT looking to make their fourth consecutive summer final."

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:33.780
"SKT trying to keep their streak of victories alive ever since we went into the League format."

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:38.220
"They have yet to lose a season. First place every 
 single time."

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:42.080
This time, it was SKT that went up 2-0.

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:48.340
But with echoes of their 2013 final, KT brought it back to game 5.

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:51.680
And this time, it was enough.

00:21:51.680 --> 00:22:25.420
(Casting)

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:32.480
KT weren’t able to defeat the ROX Tigers in finals. And, in classic KT fashion,

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:39.440
they missed out on Worlds, while SKT managed to become the first team to defend their world title.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:46.440
KT fans were exuberant for one glorious day but their win was still a footnote on the year.

00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:52.200
2016 still belonged to SKT, in the end.

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:56.720
KT fans tasted what it was like to topple their demons.

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:04.200
And the organization wanted more. So, in 2017, they returned to their early Brood War roots.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:11.500
If SKT was a problem, they would bring in the best talent the world had to offer in an attempt to defeat them.

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:16.380
"I mean let's be real how many teams in the world, do you not look at every role and be like oh I'd take the

00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:21.140
KT Rolster player over you. SKT maybe? Apart from that not many teams."

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:30.280
Smeb. Score. Pawn. Deft. Mata. Five of the best players to ever touch the game.

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:34.820
KT had crafted the roster to topple SKT.

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:37.420
They had crafted it to win.

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:43.280
WIth the return of former Samsung players to Korea, and the proven dominance of Smeb

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:50.480
and Score in their positions, it seemed like KT’s 2017 roster was too good to fail.

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:52.940
But it didn’t work.

00:23:53.440 --> 00:24:00.160
KT Made it to the finals in the spring split, but were toppled 3-0 by SKT.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:16.280
(Casting)

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:23.560
In the summer split, in Round 3 of the playoffs SKT won a closer series 3-2.

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:27.780
SKT won all four of their regular season meetings.

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:32.380
Then, KT missed out on Worlds, yet again.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:38.580
KT built their team to defeat SKT, but they couldn’t shake their demon.

00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:41.920
They were good in 2017.

00:24:41.960 --> 00:24:44.300
But they were not good enough.

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:48.880
Of course, at the end of the year, neither was SKT.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:25:00.960
(Casting)

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:03.960
Fans saw 2017 as a failure.

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:07.080
Despite results that would be incredible for another team.

00:25:07.740 --> 00:25:13.100
And SKT’s 2018 spring split was inauspicious at best.

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:17.440
After struggling to find a version of their roster that worked for much of the regular

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:21.920
season, they managed to narrowly qualify for playoffs.

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:27.320
But, for once, KT entered playoffs with the upper hand.

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:32.720
For once, KT had the head-to-head during the regular season.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:36.380
For once, KT were the favorites.

00:25:36.900 --> 00:25:41.340
KT’s season ended in defeat at the hands of the Afreeca Freecs.

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:47.520
But even though they haven’t won a domestic title since 2014, they proved that, with a

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:53.540
little help from new additions Ucal and Rush, their roster can do what it was built to do

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:55.840
win the Telecom War.

00:25:55.840 --> 00:26:26.320
(Casting)

00:26:26.680 --> 00:26:30.640
SKT and KT’s rivalry has taken different shapes

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:32.140
in different games.

00:26:32.140 --> 00:26:35.540
Generations of players and coaches have played their part.

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:41.820
But each time their players sit down in front of a keyboard to play, there’s something else there.

00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:47.980
An extra boost pushing them to play their best — to be better, always better.

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:53.720
In the end, their position in one split, or in one tournament, doesn’t matter.

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:59.760
Fans will still pack the studio when it's time for KT and SKT to face off.

00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:04.580
They’ll still scream “Fighting!” when the game begins.

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:08.600
And they will still roar with pride when their side is victorious.

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:14.880
KT Rolster and SK Telecom have been jockeying for position in esports for nearly as long

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:17.120
as esports have existed.

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:22.140
Both sides have won skirmishes, both sides have triumphed in battle.

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:26.160
But the Telecom War is far from over.

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:31.200
"SK Telecom!"

00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:37.340
"KT Rolster!"

00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:48.260
